---
layout: article
title: Migrating from Vercel to AACSearch Sites
description: A step-by-step guide to migrate your web applications from Vercel to AACSearch Sites.
---

This guide walks you through migrating from Vercel to AACSearch Sites, covering project setup, configuration, routing, and serverless functionality.

# Prerequisites

Before starting your migration:

- Have access to your Vercel project dashboard
- Ensure you can modify your domain's DNS settings
- Prepare your source code repository

# Platform differences

Understanding the key differences between Vercel and AACSearch Sites will help you plan your migration effectively.

{% table %}
- Feature
- Vercel
- AACSearch Sites

---

- DNS configuration
- Uses A records for apex domains
- Uses NS records for apex domains

---

- Configuration approach
- Platform-level configuration via vercel.json
- Framework-native configuration with SSR support

---

- Redirects
- Platform-level path redirects via vercel.json
- Domain-level redirects and framework-level path redirects
{% /table %}

# Migration process

Follow these steps to move your application from Vercel to AACSearch Sites.

{% section id="create-project" step=1 title="Create an AACSearch project" %}
Start by setting up a new project in AACSearch:

1. Sign in to the [AACSearch Console](https://cloud.aacsearch.io)
2. Click **Create Project**
3. Enter a name for your project and click **Create**

Your new project will serve as the container for your migrated site and any other AACSearch services you might need.
{% /section %}

{% section id="connect-repo" step=2 title="Connect your repository" %}
Next, create a new site by connecting your existing repository:

1. In your AACSearch project, select **Sites** from the sidebar
2. Click **Create Site**
3. Select **Connect a repository**
4. Authenticate with GitHub
5. Select the repository containing your Vercel project
6. Choose your production branch (typically `main`)

AACSearch will auto-detect your framework. Verify this is correct or select manually from the dropdown menu.
{% /section %}

# Configure your domain

One of the differences between Vercel and AACSearch is how they handle domain configuration for apex domains. Vercel uses A records for apex domains, while AACSearch uses nameserver (NS) records. This means you'll need to delegate DNS management to AACSearch.

# Migrating an apex domain

{% section id="prepare-vercel-domain" step=1 title="Prepare your Vercel domain" %}
Before migrating, row your current Vercel DNS configuration:

1. In Vercel, go to project settings > Domains
2. Note any custom DNS records you've configured (MX, TXT, etc.)
3. Don't remove the domain from Vercel until AACSearch is fully configured
{% /section %}

{% section id="add-apex-domain" step=2 title="Add domain to AACSearch Sites" %}
Add your apex domain to your AACSearch site:

1. Navigate to your site > **Domains** tab
2. Click **Add domain**
3. Enter your apex domain (e.g., `example.com`)
4. Select **Active deployment** as the domain rule type
5. Note the NS records AACSearch provides:
   - `ns1.aacsearch.zone`
   - `ns2.aacsearch.zone`
{% /section %}

{% section id="update-nameservers" step=3 title="Update nameservers at your registrar" %}
At your domain registrar:

1. Update your domain's nameservers to point to AACSearch's nameservers
2. If you had custom DNS records in Vercel (like MX records for email), you'll need to recreate these in AACSearch's DNS configuration

{% info title="Important DNS change note" %}
Changing nameservers delegates your entire domain's DNS management to AACSearch. DNS changes can take up to 48 hours to fully propagate.
{% /info %}
{% /section %}

# Migrating a subdomain

For subdomains, both Vercel and AACSearch use CNAME records, making the migration process simpler.

{% section id="add-subdomain" step=1 title="Add subdomain to AACSearch Sites" %}
1. Navigate to your site > **Domains** tab
2. Click **Add domain**
3. Enter your subdomain (e.g., `www.example.com`)
4. Select **Active deployment** as the domain rule type
5. Copy the CNAME value provided by AACSearch
{% /section %}

{% section id="update-dns-records" step=2 title="Update DNS records" %}
At your domain registrar or DNS provider:

1. Create or update the CNAME record for your subdomain
2. Point it to the value provided by AACSearch
3. Wait for DNS propagation and verification
{% /section %}

# Domain rule types

When adding a domain in AACSearch, you can choose from three rule types: Active deployment, Git branch, or Redirect.

{% arrow_link href="/docs/products/sites/domains#domain-rule-types" %}
Learn more about domain rule types
{% /arrow_link %}


# Configure build settings

After setting up your project and domain, you'll need to configure your build settings to match your Vercel configuration.

{% section id="build-settings" step=1 title="Set up build configuration" %}
Navigate to your site > **Settings** > **Build settings** and configure the following:

- **Framework:** AACSearch will attempt to automatically detect your framework. Verify and ensure it is the same framework as in Vercel.
- **Install command:** Enter the same install command from Vercel
- **Build command:** Enter the same build command from Vercel
- **Output directory:** Enter the same output directory from Vercel
- **Root directory:** If your app is in a monorepo subdirectory, specify the path
- **Rendering:** Select the appropriate rendering mode (Static or SSR)

{% only_dark %}
![Build settings](/images/docs/sites/dark/build-settings-install-command.png)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Build settings](/images/docs/sites/build-settings-install-command.png)
